          "source_quote": "καὶ τῷ ἀγνοοῦντι πλήθει προσαντιβαλλέτωσαν, ἵν’ ἐπιφαίνωνται",
          "english_quote": "and bring them to the attention of the unaware multitude so that they may become visible",
          "issue": "The note correctly acknowledges uncertainty about the action and subject, but “become visible” narrows ἐπιφαίνωνται to passive publicity. The verb can express showing oneself or coming on the scene; the purpose may concern members of the multitude coming to the sick rather than the sick becoming visible. The supplied wording does not securely establish the draft’s construal. This is a meaning problem, not demonstrated source corruption.",
          "suggested_revision": "Reassess the verb and its subject within the supplied edition. A provisional, less restrictive rendering is “and make them known to the multitude that is unaware, so that they may appear,” accompanied by a specific alternative such as “so that they may come to visit them.” Explain the possible subjects and the implied nature of “visit”; neither proposal is accepted without fresh review. Retain this as an unresolved meaning issue in source_issues rather than treating rarity as evidence of corrupt Greek.",
          "severity": "major"
        }
      ]
    },
